messages.body_plain:
Hi,
Is there a way using RServi to inject an object that can call back java
method?

I will try to describe my usecase:

>From Java code I read R source file with R6 class:
FunctionCall rtaskFile = this.fRservi.createFunctionCall("source");
rtaskFile.add("file", "file(\""+ fullPath + "\")");
rtaskFile.add("chdir", "TRUE");
rtaskFile.evalVoid(null);
The example of R6 class is here:
https://github.com/systemincloud/sic-examples/blob/dev/com.systemincloud.examples.tasks.rtask/src/test/r/break/Break.R

library(R6)
library(sicrtask)

Break <- R6Class("Break",
  inherit = RTask,
  public = list(
    rtaskinfo = RTaskInfo$new(),
    in1 = NA,
    initialize_ports = function() {
      self$in1 <- InputPort$new(name = "in1", data_type = "Int32")
    },
    initialize = function() {
      self$initialize_ports()
    },
    execute = function(grp) {
 data <- self$in1$get_data("Int32")
 i = data$values[0]
 if( i == 3 ) self$exit_runner()
}
  ),
  private = list(
  )
)

Further, in Java code I instantiate this class:
  this.fRservi.evalVoid("instance <- " + name + "$new()", null);

and during application run I can several times invoke method execute:
  this.fRservi.evalVoid("instance$execute(" + grp + ")", null);

Inside method execute there could be that R wants to read some data from
Java:
  data <- self$in1$get_data("Int32")

Is there some easy way to do it?
In Jython version of this usecase I inject a PyObject object that calls
Java method, however all is done in one JVM.
In CPython I use thrift and its two way socket communication.

I would like to reuse from statET as much as possible, e.g. I would like to
do data Java/R transformation using RObject implementations.

* My solutions *
For now I try to solve this usecase this way:
I added socket communication between Java and R:

